-Combines Lambertian diffuse shading with Local Ambient Occlusion and Soft Shadows. It computes the normal from the gradient and mixes the base color with darkened variants based on LAO and shadow contributions.
+[S1, unofficial] Combines Lambertian diffuse shading with Local Ambient Occlusion and Soft Shadows contributions to produce the final volume color. It computes the normal from the gradient and mixes the base color with darkened variants based on LAO and shadow contributions.
 
 ### Deferred Shading for Ambient Occlusion
-Renders world position and surface normal pixels to a buffer, then executes AO computation in the fragment shader using deferred shading to avoid evaluating hidden geometry.
+[S2] Renders world position and surface normal pixels of the image from the current point of view to a buffer, then executes AO computation in the fragment shader on each pixel using the underlying normal and position to avoid evaluating hidden geometry.
 
 ### Pose-Based Ambient Occlusion Approximation
-Approximates ambient occlusion by modulating Gaussian RGB color based on local body pose, handling self-occlusions and shadows from garment wrinkles. It uses an MLP to output a scaling factor $s \in [0, 2]$ that multiplies the RGB color, clipped to $[0, 1]$.
+[S3] A shading model that approximates ambient occlusion by modulating Gaussian RGB color based on local body pose, handling self-occlusions and shadows from garment wrinkles, assuming Lambertian reflectance. It uses an MLP to output a scaling factor $s \in [0, 2]$ that multiplies the RGB color of each Gaussian, which is then clipped to $[0, 1]$.
+
+### LAO Clip Plane Interaction
+[S4, unofficial] A method to handle clip planes in LAO-illuminated volumes by letting the clip plane affect sample opacity during LAO computation, avoiding the undefined gradients and low-quality illumination of diffuse shading at clip plane boundaries. Outside the clip plane, samples' opacities are reduced to zero, allowing full light contribution outside the clip plane.
 
 ## Background
-The concept of Local Ambient Occlusion in volume rendering originated from the need to enhance depth cues without the prohibitive cost of global illumination. Early implementations focused on statistical approximations of local opacity distributions [S3]. Concurrently, GPU-based techniques emerged to provide real-time LAO for volumetric data, often utilizing multiresolution structures and incremental updates [S1, unofficial][S2]. More recently, the concept has been adapted for neural rendering and Gaussian Splatting, where pose-based approximations handle self-occlusions in animatable avatars [S3].
+The concept of Local Ambient Occlusion in volume rendering originated from the need to enhance depth cues without the prohibitive cost of global illumination [S1, unofficial][S3]. Early implementations focused on statistical approximations of local opacity distributions [S3]. Concurrently, GPU-based techniques emerged to provide real-time LAO for volumetric data, often utilizing deferred shading and cone-tracing in the fragment shader [S2]. Multiresolution structures and incremental updates were later introduced to optimize performance [S4, unofficial]. More recently, the concept has been adapted for neural rendering and Gaussian Splatting, where pose-based approximations handle self-occlusions in animatable avatars [S3].
 
